package scryer:subtitle@1.1.0;

/// The subtitle-provider contract, revision 1.1.
///
/// # What is unchanged from 1.0
///
/// The payloads. Both exports still carry UTF-8 JSON: `describe` returns a
/// `PluginDescriptor` document, and `process` exchanges a
/// `scryer_plugin_sdk::command::PluginCommandRequest` for a
/// `PluginCommandResponse` carrying `PluginCommand::Subtitle` and its
/// `validate-config` / `search` / `download` / `generate` operations. A 1.0
/// guest's request and response types and its dispatch `match` are the same
/// here; only the shape of the `process` function moved. Keeping the payload an
/// opaque byte string is still what lets the SDK add subtitle operations
/// without a WIT revision.
///
/// # Why `process` is async
///
/// A subtitle invocation is almost entirely waiting on somebody else's server:
/// a catalog search, a paced sequence of detail fetches, a download that has to
/// respect a provider's rate limit. On the 1.0 world that wait had to happen
/// inside a synchronous export, so a guest could only reach the network through
/// the encoded `host-call` door — one blocking call at a time, no cancellation
/// visible to the guest, and no way to run the same pacing code the indexer
/// world's plugins already use. Lifting `process` into the canonical ABI's
/// async form lets the guest `await` the runtime's `http` and `sleep`, observe
/// the invocation deadline, and unwind when the host cancels, while the host
/// drives other instances on the same worker.
///
/// `describe` stays synchronous deliberately. It is a pure function over a
/// literal descriptor — it performs no host calls at all — and keeping it sync
/// means the host can answer a catalog listing with a plain blocking call
/// instead of standing up a concurrent task per plugin.
///
/// # Why both imports
///
/// `scryer:host/services@1.0.0` is the single door for everything encoded: one
/// postcard `host-call` covering config, plugin state, HTTP, host-owned archive
/// extraction, sockets and processes, extensible by adding an SDK variant
/// rather than revising WIT. `scryer:runtime/host@1.0.0` is the typed,
/// family-neutral surface that door cannot express — the `async` `http` and
/// `sleep`, the clocks and the invocation deadline, and the state
/// compare-and-swap the plugin-owned rate gates and quotas are built on. A
/// subtitle provider gets both, and its shared engine code (the newznab
/// protocol engine, the PDK's pacing primitives) is now linkable into it
/// because the runtime package is family-neutral. See each package for its own
/// contract.
///
/// WASI Preview 2 is imported implicitly: the host links the full p2 surface
/// (`wasmtime_wasi::p2::add_to_linker_async`), so a guest sees clocks, random,
/// and its captured stdio through `wasi:*` without those imports appearing in
/// this world. Subtitle providers receive no filesystem preopens for catalog
/// operations; a generator invocation is preopened its read-only input root
/// exactly as the legacy runtime preopened it.
world subtitle-provider {
  import scryer:host/services@1.0.0;
  import scryer:runtime/host@1.0.0;

  enum invocation-error {
    failed,
    cancelled,
    invalid-response,
  }

  export describe: func() -> list<u8>;
  export process: async func(request: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, invocation-error>;
}
